            <ArticleTitle>Enhanced recovery principles applied to revision hip and knee arthroplasty reduces length of stay and blood transfusion.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>555-560</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jor.2017.08.012</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">This is the first study reporting the application of Enhanced Recovery Principles (ERP) to revision arthroplasty.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective series of 132 revision hip and knee replacements treated with ERP.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Infiltration was associated with reduced LOS in knees (6 vs 8.5 days), lower PCA usage and incidence of transfusion in knees (2 vs 3 days) and hips (1 vs 6 days). Revisions for infection had a longer LOS (5.4 vs 11.5 days p = 0.001), a greater use of PCA and a higher incidence of transfusion (5 vs 0) in both knees and hips.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The application of ERPs to revision arthroplasty is safe. Infiltration appears to be an important factor in improving outcome measures.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
